 "answer": "Based on the provided context, the pass-rate threshold for determining a failing exit code is not set by an environment variable. Instead, it is configured within the scripts or command-line options used during the evaluation process. For example:\n\n1. In the CI/CD Integration section (context [4]), the pass-rate threshold is set at 95% within a bash script using a calculation and comparison:\n   ```bash\n   PASS_RATE=$(jq '.results.stats.successes / (.results.stats.successes + .results.stats.failures) * 100' results.json)\n   if (( $(echo \"$PASS_RATE < 95\" | bc -l) )); then\n     echo \"Quality gate failed: Pass rate ${PASS_RATE}% < 95%\"\n     exit 1\n   fi\n   ```\n\n2. Similarly, in the AWS CodeCommit Integration section (context [5]), the pass-rate threshold is also set at 95% within a YAML configuration script:\n   ```yaml\n   PASS_RATE=$(jq '.results.stats.successes / (.results.stats.successes + .results.stats.failures) * 100' promptfoo-results.json)\n   if (( $(echo \"${PASS_RATE} < 95\" | bc -l) )); then\n     echo \"Quality gate failed: ${PASS_RATE}% < 95%\"\n     exit 1\n   fi\n   ```\n\n3. In the Looper Integration section (context [7]), the threshold is similarly set at 95%:\n   ```yaml\n   test $(echo \"$RATE >= 95\" | bc) -eq 1   # fail if <95 %\n   ```\n\nThe concept of a pass-rate threshold is implemented through inline scripts rather than a specific environment variable. Therefore, as per the provided context, there is no environment variable that sets the pass-rate threshold for `promptfoo eval` to return a failing exit code.",
